</div>

<p>Though Watts’ interest in Buddhism hardly needs stating, it’s worth remembering that he chose to be ordained as an Episcopalian priest rather than as a Buddhist monk. The young Bowie, for his part, very nearly took the latter path, studying under the late Lama Chime Rinpoche, who eventually told him he’d be better off going into the music business.</p>
<p>Bowie did so, of course, but with a new “sense of transience and change, which actually became fundamental to my life and my approach to it,” as he put it decades later.&nbsp;That sense manifests even in his early work, but embracing and even pursuing impermanence — the impermanence of sounds, of fashions, of hit records, of <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2017/08/how-leonard-cohen-david-bowie-faced-death-through-their-art-a-look-at-their-final-albums.html">life itself</a> — also kept him from getting mired in the irrelevance or auto-kitsch that threatens every superstar. Given the belief that nothing lasts forever,&nbsp;“looking for the source of one’s own being becomes much more important,” as does attempting artistic or intellectual challenges at the very edge of one’s abilities.&nbsp;Alan Watts would probably have agreed. The bigger question is, did he ever hear <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4BgF7Y3q-as">“Changes”</a>?</p>
